+### The re-declare hold-off over two of the longest LeaveAll periods (#951)
+
+[#665's ruling of 2026-11-19](https://github.com/kebag-logic/milan-fpga/issues/665#issuecomment-7007273447), item 2. A LeaveAll period runs up to 1.5 times LeaveAllTime (IEEE 802.1Q-2018 10.7.4.3; Milan v1.2 Table 4.3), so a withdrawn Talker's changed destination now waits two of the longest periods.
+
+- **The constant.** `SRP_REDECLARE_CS` (`srp_mbx.c`) is twice `SRP_LEAVEALL_LONGEST_CS`, plus the JoinTime and (`CTRL_LOOP_TX_MS` + 9) / 10 as before. `SRP_LEAVEALL_LONGEST_CS` is 1.5 times `SRP_LEAVEALL_CS`, the LeaveAllTime `create_participants` configures, 1000 cs. The hold-off is 3,023 cs at 1x1/1if, 3,027 at 8x8/1if and 3,030 at two interfaces; it was 2,023, 2,027 and 2,030. A compile-time check refuses a hold-off past `held_cs`'s 16 bits, and adds no code. No figure, bound or statement moves.
+- **The images.** Twenty images were linked at base and head: the target platform's four, the size fixture's four and the CPU budget harness's twelve. In each, `srp_mbx.o` is the only linked input that differs. The hold-off no longer fits an `addi` immediate, so `li a3,2023` becomes `lui a3,0x1; addi a3,a3,-1073`, 4 bytes more in `declare_sources`. What the map places after it moves 4 bytes (`.rodata` 8, by its alignment), and sram does not move. Each image was linked again with its relocations kept, and its loadable bytes equal the built image's. Listed with every address field named by its relocation's symbol, each head image differs from its base in that instruction alone.
+- **The rows.** No code path changes: the listings above differ in the hold-off's own encoding alone. The code does move: `declare_sources` is on the hot list, so the hot code after it fetches 4 bytes later. The legs were measured on both images at 1x1/1if and 8x8/1if: the `valid-destinations` arm's three groups, `srp-talker`, `srp-talker-loss` and `srp-talker-held`, the legs the hold-off reaches, and arm A's `srp`, `backlog-owed`, `datapath-events` and `aecp`, with `aecp-largest-notice` at 1x1/1if.
+  - Every leg exits 0 on both images, with no STATED line and no pass over the share.
+  - No probe changes its verdict, of 156 (50 and 16 at 1x1/1if, 45 and 45 at 8x8/1if). Each image has arm A's seven `backlog-owed` misses ([tsn-c-stack #26](https://github.com/kebag-logic/tsn-c-stack/issues/26)) and no other.
+  - In arm A the largest difference is 0.171 ms, on a 20 ms row: `backlog-owed.service.srp.last`, 7.150 ms on the base image and 7.321 on the head's, at 1x1/1if. A group whose passes fall the same way runs the same instructions on both images: `srp` runs 860,482 at 1x1/1if.
+  - In the `valid-destinations` arm at 8x8/1if a row that waits on a timer's phase moves further: `srp.talker_loss` 184.154 to 177.088 ms, of 220, and `srp-talker-held`'s MRPDU join row 1.663 to 3.392 ms, of 49. The passes cost the same, their medians within 0.4 %. From its withdrawals on, each group's loop falls out of step with the base's by about one pass a centisecond, ahead in `srp-talker` and behind in `srp-talker-loss`. A control image was built for `srp-talker-loss`, in scratch only: the base's value, with one `nop` after its store, so that it has the head's placement. It gives `srp.talker_loss` 176.954 ms, and every declaration row of the group equals the head's to the microsecond. So the rows follow the code's placement, not the hold-off's value.
+  - `flip_grade.py --check`, at either point, `load_grade.py --check` and `shape_costs.py --check` read the same tables and print the same output on both trees.
+  - No group runs a hold-off to its end on either image, so no measured row includes one.
+
 ### What is left
 
 - The owner kept 8x8 in the Mark II split's scope, approved T_svc = 20 ms ([decision](https://github.com/kebag-logic/milan-fpga/issues/665#issuecomment-6495692075)) and put the split CPU on `sys` at 100 MHz ([decision](https://github.com/kebag-logic/milan-fpga/issues/665#issuecomment-6514866239)).
